  "name": "Tia-Piah (Kiowa Gourd Dance songs)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Song repertoire of the Kiowa Gourd Clan (Tia-Piah society), a Kiowa warrior-society dance revived at Carnegie, Oklahoma, in the mid-twentieth century and since taken up by Comanche, Cheyenne and many other nations, where a gourd dance is often held in the afternoon before a powwow. Singers seated at a large shared drum sing the society's songs while the men dance in place, shaking rattles in time with the drum and stepping toward it as the song builds. The powwow drum card stands in for the gourd-dance drum and a generic shaker card for the dancers' rattles.",
